  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 205 PM MDT Tue Sep 22 2026\n\nSynoptically, the ridge remains to the south over the Southern \nUS / Gulf region. Guidance is in fairly good agreement regarding\na shortwave moving through the region Friday through Saturday. \nHowever, some uncertainty remains as to how far south into the \nTri-State area it will extend. Sunday through Monday, flow will \nbe predominately zonal. Any shortwaves passing through the area \nwill help increase precipitation chances. \n\nHigh temperatures for the week look to remain in the mid to \nupper 80s for the majority of the Tri-State area, though both \nFriday and Tuesday will be a tad bit \"cooler\" with highs in the \n70s. Friday holds the best precipitation chances for the long \nterm period, with the Probability of Precipitation (PoPs) in the\n20-60% range. The highest amounts are expected to be the \neastern portions of the Tri- State area, as the upper level \nshortwave moves through. For the remainder of the period. PoPs \nstay around 15-30% as various afternoon showers move through. \nFriday is currently forecast to be the windiest day of the long \nterm period, with sustained winds around 10-20 mph and gusts up \nto 35 mph. Areas most likely to experience these winds will be \neast of the CO/KS border and south of I-70, primarily during the\nmorning hours on Friday. \n\nTuesday into Wednesday next week look to be the most notable \nday of the long term period, though it is about seven days out \nand a fair amount can change. Currently, the Canadian-NH, ECMWF,\nand GFS models all show around a 571 dam upper-level trough \nmoving through the Tri- State area, accompanied by a jet streak.\nThe main uncertainty lies in how far south the trough tracks. \nIt could pass as for south as the CO/KS/OK border or as far \nnorth as the CO/NE border. Additionally, all three deterministic\nmodels indicate some form of a surface low on Tuesday, but with\nlittle agreement regarding its location. With all of that being\nsaid, this could be our next precipitation system and will \ndefinitely be on our radar to keep and eye on.",
